From: pcm@cs.brown.edu (Peter C. McCluskey)
Subject: ECON: Tragedy of the commons
X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute freely

 szabo@techbook.com  (Nick Szabo) writes:
>Perhaps we could have "genetic rights", which might be a sort 
>of cross between genetic patents and mineral rights.  A piece
>of real estate might be sold with a deed so that the conservation 
>group, biotech company, or other owner of the genetic right maintains
>a specific  claim on habitat space for their species.  This would

 Development rights to pieces of land are already sold separately
from the rest of the rights in some places. But tailoring them to
individual species would be difficult.

>The amount of undiscovered biodiversity, and how fast it is 
>decreasing are major scientific controversies.  Directly through

 It's a political controversy. There's little science involved.

>regulation, and perhaps indirectly through lost genetic
>information, this controversy costs the world economy $billions
>per year.  Robin Hanson's idea for idea futures looks more
>brilliant all the time; here we could set up bets on, for 
>example, the amount of documented genetic diversity in 2000,

 An interesting idea, but I fear that "documented genetic diversity" would
end up meaning a count of species. What this quantifies is primarily the
effort that has been spent on discovering new species. It also has the
problem that "species" is undefined for organisms that reproduce asexually
and contains some ambiguities for sexually reproducing organisms.
 I think that this controversy might be moved to a higher level if people
were aware of the definition of diversity used in ecology texts (at least
it was used in the mid 70s when I was an undergrad) of 1 - sum(p(i)^2), where
p(i) is the number of organisms of species i divided by the total number of
organisms (i.e. rare species are insignificant). Not an overly useful
definition, but it would help show how arbitrary the popular one is.
