Date: Wed, 17 Mar 93 21:53:55 EST
From: sulko-m@acsu.buffalo.edu (Mark A. Sulkowski)
Subject: PHIL: Was Patrick Henry a deathist?

From: Carol Moore <cmoore@cap.gwu.edu>
>This seems like an appropriate place to
>ask something I've wondered about:
>Is Patrick Henry's famous phrase:
>"Give me liberty or give me death"  --
>horrors-- "deathist"?

	No, I don't believe so.  Patrick Henry was not saying
that death was good.  He was saying that his life would not
be worth living under tyranny.

	Today he might have said:

	"Give me liberty, or give me a cryonic suspension!"

	:^)


>Is defending
>someone else's liberty even unto death 
>MORE deathist that defending one's own.
>Just wondering.

	Well... if we are to take a look at Bruce Benson's analysis
of customary law, let's say you have joined a reciprocal support group
where there are expectations of mutual support in legal matters.
You would be expected to lend your support to help defend the liberty
of your fellow group members or face being ostracized.  Since the
benefits of loyalty outweigh the penalties of ostracism, I conclude
that it is not more deathist.

	Ask a silly question...

	More seriously: deathism is, in a sense, the idea that
physical immortality of the kinds that extropians desire is wrong.
Death (meaning a short life) is seen as somehow natural or desirable.
Some people believe it is good because then they get to go to Heaven.
Others think it is good because what is natural (like our genetically
determined lifespan) is good by definition.  Others, like humanists
such as Isaac Asimov, rationalize that death is good because it makes
room for future generations.  Others, like the woman from China I
mentioned in a previous post, think death is good because it allows
one to learn from other incarnations.  Etc.

	I am an immortalist.  I want to live forever in some form
or other.  This form may change from time to time.  I am not a
deathist because I think death is bad.  I don't relish the idea
of personal extinction.  I would consider dying if it were the only
possible way to avoid something like torture.  This would not mean
that I suddenly thought of death as 'good', but rather it would be
the 'lesser of two evils'.  Does this make sense?  Patrick Henry
*may* have been a deathist, but not because of his quote.

