Date: Wed, 17 Mar 1993 23:04:28 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: reason

Kryz:
> (i) Existence of a society results in protection of irrationla people by
> the rational ones.

Do you mean "state" here?  Anyway, it doesn't matter.  Many kinds
of functional delusions and efficient irrationality function in both
statist and PPL arenas.  Furthermore, most attempts to define
"rational" behavior are themselves based on vastly simplistic,
irrational assumptions. We simply don't have the computational ability 
to be fully rational, and it is not clear what the best allocation
betwen rational and irrational thought is.

Another example of efficient irrationality: Newtonian physics
is literally false, but still the most widely used physics in
engineering, due to its simplicity.  And this is in physics,
where we can set up models much closer to reality than is
possible in biological and social interactions.  The most
efficient "engineering" models for social interactions are
likely to be quite a distance from the literal truth, and they
are likely to rely heavily on the "previous art" (epigenetic
propensities, customs, lore, etc.)

> you theorize logic on principle, and experiment
> with it in consequences.

Such theorizing is often inefficient and impractical compared to
less rational options (eg follow instincts, mimic a role model,
use poetic metaphors, etc.)  Such reasoning also often occurs in an 
empirically starved environment and ends up failing miserably in the 
real world.  On this forum, in academia, etc.  we have a good 
opportunity to explore these theories, but we shouldn't expect them to 
be the most practical ways to live.

> Nota bene, an altruist  m u s t  reject reason, as it is the means of
> individual survival, and altruists preach that people are to prey on 
> each other in search of sacrifice.

Plenty of irrational people survive -- and what's further propagate,
which is rather necessary since no individual has ever survived
for over c. 125 year.  The fact that you distilled "reason" to act
solely in the service of  "individual survival" is itself vastly 
oversimplified. That isn't meant as a personal attack; 
I'm just pointing out that everybody makes those kinds of major 
oversimplifications.

(What difference life extension makes for the rich & motivated minority will 
make in the future remains to be worked out).  Rational, empirical 
evidence indicates that people holding some kinds of irrational basic 
beliefs (eg Hutterites, Mormons, some Islamic sects) both survive longer 
and propagate more widely than people holding more rational beliefs (eg 
agnostics) while many other kinds of irrational beliefs (eg many other
Christian sects) lead to shorter survival and less propagation. It may 
be efficient live life irrationally and ignore these facts, but on this 
forum we have an opportunity to explore these issues in a deeper, more 
empirical, more rational way.

>[altruists are irrational]

You fail to distinguish between reciprocal altruism, philanthropy,
altruism with other people's money, etc.  Many of
these are often in the "rational self-interest of the individual"
in practice but not in abstract theory, because the abstract theory
leaves out necessary detail, such as the positive effects 
philanthropy can have on reputation.

Political action against altruistic motives is often itself 
genuine altruism, since the political activist spends much more 
effort to create a public good than is received in 
improvement of the altruist's  political environment.

For example, Libertarian Party activists are for more altruistic,
ie genuinely self-sacrificing, than the typical American who favors
welfare etc.  

